{"kind":"request","text":"codex: build the inventory application in its own repository. Hugh asked for this today and chose the parallel route deliberately after I put the sequencing options to him.\n\nWHAT HE SAID, and it is a fair criticism of where we are: we have a design note for the chess refactor, but we also need a concrete application for the inventory app itself, per notes/2026-08-26-jsonata-ddl-application-implementation.md. He named the location and the remote: build in ~/play/gitseq-inventory, link it to https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq-inventory.git.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY EXISTS TODAY, because I checked rather than assuming, and it is less than the board implies. The inventory application exists only as a spike inside gitseq: application.sql at 34 lines, inventory.jsonata at 18, and cmd/jsonata-inventory/main.go at 56. One hundred and eight lines in total. That was enough to exercise application-bound Git replay into a disposable SQLite projection, which is what spikes one and two needed from it, and it is not an application. I searched the whole durable projection: thirty-seven statements mention inventory and not one of them scopes a standalone application. There has never been a request for this.\n\nThe repository state matches. /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-inventory is a bare git init with no commits, no remote and no refs/seq. The GitHub repository exists and git ls-remote returns zero refs, so it is empty and waiting rather than missing.\n\nWHY NOW RATHER THAN AFTER SPIKE THREE. I offered hugh three routes and recommended this one. The SQL, the fold and the driver are already merged and reviewed, so nothing blocks a genesis. Spike three is gated behind spike one, which is open, unpromised, and sitting behind six other rows on claude's queue; waiting for it could mean waiting a long time for work that is not actually blocking. The cost of the parallel route is honest and belongs in condition (7): spike three may still narrow the SQL surface, and anything you assume from it may have to change. Name your assumptions so we can see what is exposed.\n\nI am amending spike three separately so its inventory UI is built in the new repository rather than under spike/. That lane stays yours and stays gated; this request does not unblock it and does not include the UI.\n\nON THE DURABLE LOG, because we have been bitten here before. Chess was pushed with a synced main and no refs/seq, and the durable log simply was not there. Condition (3) asks you to prove the push with ls-remote output rather than assert it.\n\nI have addressed this to you rather than claude for two reasons: you bootstrapped the chess workroom and know the procedure, and claude currently holds six open rows plus the decision-records repair that has blocked its own lane all session. If you would rather not take it, decline with an assert and ask me to retire this and refile to claude; I would rather it sat visibly with the right owner than quietly with the wrong one.","body":{"conditions":"Hugh authorised this today and chose to bootstrap now, in parallel with the remaining spikes, rather than wait for spike three. Deliver the inventory application as its own repository and its own workroom, following the chess pattern. (1) Build in /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-inventory, which is an empty git init with no commits, no remote and no refs/seq. Set origin to https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq-inventory.git, which I verified exists and carries zero refs. (2) Bootstrap its own workroom with gs init: its own genesis, its own roster, its own durable log. It is not a worktree or a branch of gitseq. Add hugh, planner, claude and codex to the roster with the roles the chess workroom uses, and state in the artifact which roles you granted and why. (3) Push refs/seq explicitly and prove it: a synced main does NOT carry the durable log, and the chess repo has been caught in exactly this state. Show git ls-remote output listing both the main ref and the refs/seq ref after pushing. (4) Carry the reviewed inventory material out of gitseq's spike tree as the application's starting point: spike/jsonataddl/inventory/application.sql (34 lines), spike/jsonataddl/inventory/folds/inventory.jsonata (18 lines), and the driver in spike/cmd/jsonata-inventory/main.go (56 lines). State clearly in the artifact what you carried verbatim, what you adapted and why. Do not silently rewrite the DDL or the fold; if the profile in notes/2026-08-26-jsonata-ddl-application-implementation.md forbids something they currently do, report that as a finding rather than quietly fixing it. (5) The DDL profile is a condition, not a suggestion: CREATE TABLE, CREATE INDEX and CREATE VIEW with primary keys, uniqueness, foreign keys and checks; no ALTER, triggers, virtual tables, attached databases, PRAGMAs or extension loading. Types limited to TEXT, INTEGER, REAL, BLOB, BOOLEAN and JSON, money as minor-unit integers, timestamps as an explicitly checked TEXT or INTEGER convention, integers inside JSON's exactly representable range. (6) The boundary from the note is a condition: failure is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface, and is NOT grounds to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or to enlarge the minimal application. If the application needs the kernel to learn about the database, stop and file it as a finding. (7) Do not depend on anything spike three has not yet proven. The determinism corpus and the frontier-wait semantics are still open, so state explicitly which behaviours you are assuming and which you have demonstrated, and keep the assumed set as small as you can. Anything you assume is a thing that may have to change. (8) Scope this first head to a working application a reader can run: schema, fold, the replay-into-SQLite path, and a README with one path a new user can follow end to end. UI is not in this head. (9) Gates green in the new repo at the exact head with -count=1: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1. Per-path artifacts at every stable path, filed in the inventory workroom, not this one. (10) Independent review inside the inventory workroom recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge until that review is approved and ratified. (11) Report back here with the origin URL, the genesis id, the exact head, and the artifact ids, so the two workrooms can be read together.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}